      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "10,975 diagnostic left-heart catheterizations, performed between 1968 and 1987 in 8,436 men and 2,539 women, were analysed regarding indication, results and therapeutic implications. 6,974 patients (64%) had coronary heart disease, 2,573 (23%) a valvar defect and 849 a cardiomyopathy. The proportion of patients with coronary heart disease increased from 21% to 74% during the 20-year period, while that of patient with valvar defects fell from 52% to under 19%. Severe cardiac complications in relation to the procedure occurred in 0.88%, ending fatally in 8 patients (0.07%). 61% of patients with coronary heart disease and 31% of those with valvar defects were treated conservatively. Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) gradually became more common after its introduction in 1977. In 1987, PTCA was indicated as often as a bypass operation."},
